  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 129 AM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nThe mid-level ridging slowly nudges eastward with time from this \nweekend into early next week as a trough slowly progresses across \nthe Desert Southwest before ejecting towards the northeast. This \ntrough on today's medium range guidance ejects even farther to the \nnorth in comparison to yesterday's deterministic runs. This is a \ntrend towards a weaker upper level pattern, which becomes less \nsupportive for a front to make it across the region sometime early \nnext week. This was signaled within quite a bit of the ensemble \nguidance and thus I'm not surprised at this trend. The front may \nstill attempt to approach but has higher likelihood to get hung up \nnorth of the region with only modest push from a weaker trailing \nsecondary shortwave on the backside of the main trough. This weak \ntrailing shortwave and perhaps the front's proximity if it's close \nenough may still provide low to medium rain chances (15-40%). The \nweekend through early next week does still see an increase in \nhumidity nonetheless with an influx of low-level gulf moisture with \na return and increase in an east-southeasterly flow at the surface \ninto the low levels. Temperatures won't budge much with slightly \nmore humid nights with an increasing coverage of nocturnal and \nmorning low clouds.\n\nThere is considerable uncertainty at this time range but there may \nbe some additional rain chances towards or just beyond the end of \nour official forecast period with some potential interactions with \nmoisture from the Eastern Pacific with Tropical Storm Vance and the \narrival of a front that some of the model guidance haven shown to \npick up on. Continue to check in as the pattern evolves and when \nconfidence starts to increase with time.",
